WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.700
This lesson introduces the workspace you will use
throughout the course.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:12.895
We will create a diagram, choose an editing view, navigate
the canvas, and find the settings that make the editor
comfortable to use.

00:00:13.015 --> 00:00:16.315
The example is a customer portal launch.

00:00:16.435 --> 00:00:19.910
You do not need Plant UML experience yet.

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:27.005
Your first goal is to recognize where a command belongs
and distinguish a change to your view from a change to the
document.

00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:34.308
The diagram chooser offers seven diagram types.

00:00:34.428 --> 00:00:40.978
Gantt is for schedules, WBS for the breakdown of work, and
Activity for workflows and decisions.

00:00:41.098 --> 00:00:44.448
Sequence explains interactions over time.

00:00:44.568 --> 00:00:52.443
Use Case connects actors to capabilities, Class describes
types and members, and Component describes software
structure.

00:00:52.563 --> 00:00:59.488
Choose a type for the question you want to answer, rather
than trying to put every kind of information into one
picture.

00:00:59.608 --> 00:01:03.333
The visual tools and inspectors adapt to your choice.

00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:10.408
Start with controls how much source is provided.

00:01:10.528 --> 00:01:16.403
Blank gives you a minimal document; a simple starter gives
you a small editable example.

00:01:16.523 --> 00:01:20.823
The example route offers more realistic starting material.

00:01:20.943 --> 00:01:24.793
Give the diagram a useful name before choosing its type.

00:01:24.913 --> 00:01:28.538
We will create a Gantt starter called Portal practice.

00:01:28.658 --> 00:01:34.558
The name helps you find the diagram later, while saving
still chooses the destination file.

00:01:34.678 --> 00:01:38.903
A starter is a beginning you can change, not a locked
template.

00:01:42.433 --> 00:01:45.433
The ribbon groups commands by purpose.

00:01:45.553 --> 00:01:49.728
Edit contains history, selection and source operations.

00:01:49.848 --> 00:01:53.573
Insert creates objects for the current diagram type.

00:01:53.693 --> 00:01:59.618
Plan contains scheduling tools for Gantt, and connected-
work actions for WBS.

00:01:59.738 --> 00:02:03.988
View contains navigation, panels and diagram settings.

00:02:04.108 --> 00:02:07.333
You can collapse the ribbon when you want more room.

00:02:07.453 --> 00:02:16.803
If a command is unavailable, inspect its context before
assuming something is wrong: the diagram type, selection
and editing permission can all matter.

00:02:20.333 --> 00:02:24.783
The Plan category is still present when a Class diagram is
active.

00:02:24.903 --> 00:02:30.078
Its message explains that planning tools are for Gantt and
work breakdown diagrams.

00:02:30.198 --> 00:02:35.448
Insert instead exposes the Class editing commands
appropriate to this model.

00:02:35.568 --> 00:02:40.743
A command category can explain its scope without changing
the authored diagram.

00:02:44.267 --> 00:02:47.742
Code view gives the source editor the workspace.

00:02:47.862 --> 00:02:50.937
Diagram view gives the picture more room.

00:02:51.057 --> 00:02:57.357
Split view keeps the two together, which is useful while
learning how a visual edit changes the text.

00:02:57.477 --> 00:03:00.577
Switching views does not rewrite your plan.

00:03:00.697 --> 00:03:07.422
Compare the same tasks in all three layouts, and return to
Split when you want to check both representations.

00:03:07.542 --> 00:03:14.242
Later lessons will sometimes focus on the diagram for
interaction, and sometimes on the source for precision.

00:03:17.767 --> 00:03:21.317
Fit adjusts the preview to the available canvas.

00:03:21.437 --> 00:03:26.862
Zoom changes magnification, and panning changes which part
of the drawing you see.

00:03:26.982 --> 00:03:29.607
These are viewing operations.

00:03:29.727 --> 00:03:36.652
In a Gantt chart, timeline scale is different: it is an
authored setting that changes the time axis in the source.

00:03:36.772 --> 00:03:44.472
Use the preview controls when a diagram is difficult to
see, rather than altering dates or durations to make it
fit the screen.

00:03:44.592 --> 00:03:47.492
You can always return to a fitted view.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.175
Select a task to open its inspector.

00:03:54.295 --> 00:04:00.395
An inspector explains the object you selected and provides
its supported editing controls.

00:04:00.515 --> 00:04:06.965
When the panel takes too much space, focus on the diagram
or restore Split view using the view buttons.

00:04:07.085 --> 00:04:12.510
The Diagram options group contains filters and move policy
for the Gantt preview.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:17.580
A filter can hide work from the canvas without deleting it
from the plan.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:22.450
Close panels when you have finished a task so the
workspace stays readable.

00:04:25.967 --> 00:04:30.467
Open Settings to adjust the app appearance and editing
preferences.

00:04:30.587 --> 00:04:34.262
Light, dark and system themes affect the application.

00:04:34.382 --> 00:04:42.107
Preview adaptation changes how a drawing is presented in
the app, while a diagram theme is part of the diagram's
own appearance.

00:04:42.227 --> 00:04:47.927
Editor font size, tab size, wrapping and line numbers help
you read and edit source.

00:04:48.047 --> 00:04:51.222
Default view and zoom apply to new tabs.

00:04:51.342 --> 00:04:57.917
Set these for comfort; you do not need to change the
project itself to make the interface easier to use.

00:05:01.433 --> 00:05:04.708
Choose defaults for future work in Settings.

00:05:04.828 --> 00:05:12.403
Set Split as the default view, one hundred percent as the
default zoom, two space tabs and the chooser on startup.

00:05:12.523 --> 00:05:16.773
Apply, reopen Settings and verify those retained values.

00:05:16.893 --> 00:05:21.768
Search Help for dependency and keep the Gantt-specific
reference in view.

00:05:21.888 --> 00:05:27.088
Application defaults and Help navigation leave this
authored plan unchanged.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:35.975
Commands helps when you know what you want to do but
cannot remember where its control lives.

00:05:36.095 --> 00:05:40.345
Search for a familiar action and inspect the available
result.

00:05:40.465 --> 00:05:45.565
Help provides a quick tour, diagram-specific guidance and
keyboard shortcuts.

00:05:45.685 --> 00:05:49.985
Search there when you need an explanation rather than an
operation.

00:05:50.105 --> 00:05:57.305
Learning a few shortcuts is useful, but understanding the
command's context matters more than memorizing a long
list.

00:05:57.425 --> 00:06:02.200
The next lesson will use these editing surfaces to make
precise changes.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:13.900
If you repeatedly create the same kind of diagram, save a
personal starter rather than copying an unrelated project
each time.

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:18.770
File contains Save as starter for preserving a useful
starting source.

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:24.340
Give it a name that explains its purpose, then find
personal starters in the chooser.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:30.110
Review its source, choose Create from starter, and verify
the new independent diagram.

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:38.480
A starter helps create new work; the portable file is
where you keep the specific project's diagrams, settings
and retained history.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.275
Pause and create a separate practice diagram with a useful
name.

00:06:46.395 --> 00:06:50.770
Switch through Code, Split and Diagram views, then return
to Split.

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:56.665
Zoom in, pan to inspect another part of the drawing, and
use Fit to recover the overview.

00:06:56.785 --> 00:07:00.835
Find the editor settings and choose a readable font size.

00:07:00.955 --> 00:07:04.905
Finally, locate a command and its explanation in Help.

00:07:05.025 --> 00:07:11.425
You have succeeded when you can do those things without
changing the diagram source simply to adjust the view.

00:07:14.933 --> 00:07:21.933
Begin with the right diagram type, choose a view for the
task, and use navigation controls to see the work clearly.

00:07:22.053 --> 00:07:26.953
Use inspectors for supported object edits and the ribbon
for commands.

00:07:27.073 --> 00:07:33.623
Keep view preferences separate from authored content, and
use Help when the meaning of a control is unclear.

00:07:33.743 --> 00:07:40.293
In the next lesson you will edit a plan visually and in
source, checking that both describe the same result.
